<!-- WHAT IS TITLE INSURANCE -->
<section id="what-is">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head center reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">The essentials</span>
      <h2>What is title insurance — and why do I need it?</h2>
      <p class="lead">Many buyers overlook it, but title insurance protects both you and your lender from losses stemming from defects in the property's title.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <div class="card reveal" data-tilt>
        <div class="card__icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 2l7 4v6c0 5-3.5 8-7 10-3.5-2-7-5-7-10V6l7-4z"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Why it matters</h3>
        <p>Defects can range from improperly recorded documents to forgery, easements, or encroachments. If someone claims ownership or a defect is discovered, title insurance shields you from the resulting losses — potentially hundreds of thousands of dollars, and even your home.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card reveal" data-d="1" data-tilt>
        <div class="card__icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 1v22M17 5H9.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 0 0 7h5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 0 7H6"/></svg></div>
        <h3>How much it costs</h3>
        <p>A one-time, up-front fee — not an ongoing expense. An owner's policy is based on the home's price; a lender's on the loan amount. Together they usually cost about 0.5%–1.0% of the purchase price — roughly $1,500–$3,000 on a $300,000 home, per ALTA.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card reveal" data-d="2" data-tilt>
        <div class="card__icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M16 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H6a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="9" cy="7" r="4"/><path d="M22 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-3-3.87"/></svg></div>
        <h3>Who pays</h3>
        <p>The buyer pays for the lender's policy as part of closing costs. Either buyer or seller can pay for the owner's policy — local custom often decides. Buying both at once can reduce the owner's cost through a "simultaneous issue" charge.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card reveal" style="margin-top:1.5rem;text-align:center;background:var(--bg-tint);border:none">
      <p style="color:var(--slate);max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><strong>In North &amp; South Carolina, the price for title insurance is the same no matter which company you use.</strong> So it pays to choose a reputable company with experienced underwriters. Estimate your cost with the <a href="index.html#calculator" style="color:var(--teal-700);font-weight:600;text-decoration:underline">rate calculator</a> on our home page.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- COVERAGE ACCORDIONS -->
<section id="coverage" style="background:var(--bg-soft)">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head center reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Coverage</span>
      <h2>What title insurance does — and doesn't — cover.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-2" style="align-items:start">
      <div class="acc reveal">
        <div class="acc__item open">
          <button class="acc__head">What an owner's policy protects you against <span class="ico"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></span></button>
          <div class="acc__body" style="max-height:600px">
            <div class="acc__body-inner">
              <p style="margin-bottom:.6rem">A title policy covers underlying issues with a property's title that might have been missed before you bought the home — for example, if a public-record search failed to catch a lien or ownership dispute.</p>
              <div class="pill-list">
                <span>Property survey errors</span><span>Boundary disputes</span><span>Errors on the deed</span>
                <span>Prior building-code violations</span><span>Conflicting wills</span><span>Claims by an ex-spouse</span>
                <span>Forged documents</span><span>Contractor &amp; tax liens</span><span>Encroachments</span>
                <span>Improperly recorded documents</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="acc reveal" data-d="1">
        <div class="acc__item">
          <button class="acc__head">What title insurance does <em>not</em> cover <span class="ico"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></span></button>
          <div class="acc__body">
            <div class="acc__body-inner">
              <p>Title insurance doesn't protect against every possible infringement on your property rights. It won't cover problems caused by your own actions — such as failing to pay a contractor who replaced your roof, or not paying your property taxes. It also doesn't protect against eminent domain, when a government seizes private property for a public purpose. In short, it doesn't protect against issues newly created after you buy — it protects against issues that existed before, and might have affected your decision to purchase had you known.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="acc__item">
          <button class="acc__head">Is it really required? <span class="ico"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></span></button>
          <div class="acc__body">
            <div class="acc__body-inner">
              <p>Title insurance isn't just important — a lender's policy is required to protect their interest in the property. And an owner's policy is strongly recommended: without it, you could face significant financial liability if a defect or ownership claim surfaces. Learn more about standard vs. expanded coverage by getting in touch with our team.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="acc__item">
          <button class="acc__head">Standard vs. expanded coverage <span class="ico"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></span></button>
          <div class="acc__body">
            <div class="acc__body-inner">
              <p>Homeowners can choose between standard and expanded coverage, which broadens protection against certain additional risks. We're happy to walk you through the differences and help you decide what's right for your situation — just reach out.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

